# IdentityHashMap

## Definition

`IdentityHashMap` is a **hash table based implementation** of the `Map` interface that compares **keys using reference equality (`==`) instead of logical equality (`equals()`)**.

This is the biggest difference between `HashMap` and `IdentityHashMap`.

---

# Hierarchy

```text
                  Map
                   │
             AbstractMap
                   │
          IdentityHashMap
```

---

# Why was IdentityHashMap Introduced?

Normally in a `HashMap`

```java
String s1 = new String("Java");
String s2 = new String("Java");
```

Although

```text
s1 != s2
```

their contents are equal.

```java
s1.equals(s2)
```

returns

```text
true
```

Therefore

```java
HashMap<String,Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

map.put(s1,1);
map.put(s2,2);
```

Result

```text
{Java=2}
```

Only **one entry** exists because `HashMap` considers the keys equal.

---

Sometimes we want to distinguish objects by their **memory address (reference)** instead of their content.

For example:

```java
Student s1 = new Student(101);
Student s2 = new Student(101);
```

Even though both students contain the same data,

they are **different objects**.

In such cases Java provides

```text
IdentityHashMap
```

---

# Internal Structure

Internally it is based on a **hash table**, just like `HashMap`.

However, there is one important difference.

Instead of using

```text
hashCode()

+

equals()
```

it uses

```text
System.identityHashCode()

+

==
```

---

# What is System.identityHashCode()?

Normally

```java
obj.hashCode()
```

can be overridden.

Example

```java
class Student{

    @Override
    public int hashCode(){

        return 100;
    }
}
```

HashMap will use

```text
100
```

---

IdentityHashMap ignores overridden `hashCode()`.

Instead it uses

```java
System.identityHashCode(obj)
```

which is based on the **object's identity (reference)**.

---

# Internal Working

Suppose

```java
IdentityHashMap<String,Integer> map =
        new IdentityHashMap<>();
```

Create

```java
String s1 = new String("Java");

String s2 = new String("Java");
```

Memory

```text
Heap

s1 ─────► "Java"

s2 ─────► "Java"
```

Different objects.

Now

```java
map.put(s1,1);

map.put(s2,2);
```

IdentityHashMap checks

```text
s1 == s2
```

Result

```text
false
```

Therefore

both entries are stored.

Result

```text
{Java=1, Java=2}
```

Even though the contents are the same.

---

# Internal Algorithm

## put()

```text
Key

↓

System.identityHashCode()

↓

Bucket

↓

Compare using ==

↓

Same Reference ?

      │
 ┌────┴────┐
 │         │
Yes       No
 │         │
Update   New Entry
```

---

## get()

```text
Key

↓

System.identityHashCode()

↓

Bucket

↓

Compare References (==)

↓

Return Value
```

---

## remove()

```text
Key

↓

Find Same Reference

↓

Delete Entry
```

---

# Time Complexity

| Operation     | Complexity |
| ------------- | ---------- |
| put()         | O(1)       |
| get()         | O(1)       |
| remove()      | O(1)       |
| containsKey() | O(1)       |

Worst Case

```text
O(n)
```

